    <p>The table makes the tradeoff plain: the manual wins on noise and cleanup, the electric wins on ease of starting. A parent who uses an aspirator twice a night will feel the 60-second cleanup difference every time. A parent who cannot stand mouth suction will never pick up the NoseFrida, however fast it resets.</p>

    <h2>What about the other electrics on our shortlist?</h2>
    <p>Two more electric models sit on our shortlist, and both matter to this comparison — but neither has been tested by us, so everything below is manufacturer spec, not our measurement.</p>

    <h3>Momcozy BreezyClear (electric, spray + suction — not tested)</h3>
    <p>Momcozy's BreezyClear is a 2-in-1 spray-and-suction electric aspirator. Per the manufacturer's specs: 4 adjustable suction levels up to 65 kPa, light and music soothing, marketed as quiet and portable. We have not tested this model, so we have no cleaning-time or sound data for it. "Marketed as quiet" is a claim on a product page, not a decibel reading.</p>
    <p>One caveat before you buy it for a young baby: Momcozy lists this model for ages 2–12. For a baby under 2, the manufacturer says to follow your pediatrician's advice.</p>
