    <h2>How to decide in one minute</h2>
    <p><strong>Ask yourself one question first: does mouth suction bother you?</strong> If the answer is yes — really yes, not "maybe" — stop comparing and buy an electric. The NoseFrida's advantages do not matter if you will not use it. The Grownsy is the only electric we have measured, and it is a solid pick: quiet on its lowest setting, easy to start one-handed.</p>
    <p><strong>If mouth suction does not bother you, count the steps at 3AM.</strong> The NoseFrida resets in about half the time, makes no noise, and has nothing to charge or dry. The only recurring cost is filters. For most parents, that is the better deal — which is why it is our top pick on the shortlist.</p>
    <p><strong>Do not buy on the word "quiet."</strong> "Quiet," "whisper-quiet," and "low noise" appear on almost every electric aspirator listing and mean nothing without a measurement — and a measurement only counts next to the suction level it was taken at. Our 46 dB is SP-1, the Grownsy's lowest setting. If a listing gives no number, treat its quietness as unproven. That is exactly how we treat the Momcozy and the Braun here.</p>

    <h2>FAQ</h2>
    <h3>Is the NoseFrida better than an electric nasal aspirator?</h3>
    <p>For most parents, yes — on the things we measured. In our timed test the NoseFrida was completely silent and reset in about 60–90 seconds, against about 120–180 seconds for the tested electric Grownsy. But "better" stops there if mouth suction is a deal-breaker for you: an electric you will actually use beats a manual you will not.</p>
    <h3>Is mouth suction with the NoseFrida hygienic?</h3>
    <p>The NoseFrida uses a disposable hygiene filter between the collection chamber and the mouthpiece, replaced with every use. That is the design's answer to the hygiene question — and also its ongoing cost. Follow the manufacturer's filter instructions; we test handling and cleanup, not medical safety.</p>
    <h3>Are electric nasal aspirators too loud for a sleeping baby?</h3>
    <p>Not necessarily. The Grownsy measured about 46 dB on its lowest setting in our test — roughly the level of a quiet room. Higher settings are louder, and the vibration next to a baby's face matters as much as the number. The only aspirator guaranteed silent is a manual one.</p>
    <h3>Which is easier to clean: NoseFrida or electric?</h3>
    <p>The NoseFrida, in our testing: about 60–90 seconds to reset, against about 120–180 seconds for the Grownsy, which has more parts plus a warning card (switch off immediately, keep liquid out, keep the chamber dry). We have no cleaning times for the Momcozy or the Braun — we have not tested them.</p>
    <h3>Do I need to buy anything else with either type?</h3>
    <p>For the NoseFrida, yes: disposable hygiene filters are part of every use and are an ongoing cost. For electric models, budget for spare silicone tips and the time to dry the chamber after each use. The Braun ships with two tip sizes, which is useful for a newborn.</p>

